// The cotangent of a read, answered from the **type**.
//
// `pick` reads one element of its parameter, so the cotangent of that parameter is an array of its
// shape with one slot filled. `transpose` answers one cotangent per parameter and passes nothing else
// -- every parameter is a linear input (design principles 9.9) -- so the transposed function is handed
// the cotangent and *no* value of `x`.
//
// The shape that zero array needs is not a value: it is a fact `x: float[3]` states, and it reaches
// the law as a **known operand**. `rulesets/transpose.cml#idx_arr_t` names one port more than
// `index_into` has, that port is `extent_of::<X>()` -- the extents the type states, written into the
// graph as an `int[]` where the step is met
// (`modules/transforms/rule_expand.cpp#statedExtentsValue`, the one reading of
// `camel::core::type::statedExtentsOf`) -- and the law builds its zeros with `zeros_of(ct,
// extent_product(at))`. So the answer rests on `extent_of`, not on an array standing in for `x`
// (author, 2026-11-03 17:55, and 2026-11-04: the walk's zero carrier is removed).
//
// The entry in `test/plans/feat/compile/transforms.plan.toml` still carries this file's old name,
// because the refusal below is what a program of this shape met until the type's shape reached the
// law. Before that, the difference reached the run as a count, and a count is not a report:
//
//   error[...]: Pass 'std::nvm' failed with an internal error: Runtime indirect call norm-arity
//   mismatch: caller graph 'main' node 3 has 1 args, target graph 'transpose_of_pick' has 2 norm
//   ports.
//   help: ... which is a defect of the compiler rather than of the program. ... report the program
//   together with the pass list.
//
// on `std::nvm`, whose help asked a reader to report their own program as a defect of the compiler,
// and an abort on the fast VM (`FastVM indirect call norm-arity mismatch for graph
// 'transpose_of_pick': expected 2, got 1`, exit 134). The entry still asserts that neither of those
// sentences comes back; what it answers now is `([0.0, 1.0, 0.0])` -- the adjoint of `x[1]` at
// ct = 1 fills slot 1 and every other slot is zero.
//
// A parameter whose type states **no** extents (`float[...]`) is the other side of the same rule: no
// law can state the length, so the build refuses by name
// (`RuntimeDiag::TransposeNeedsParameterShape`, pinned by `transpose_needs_parameter_shape`), and a
// law that reads an *element* of the argument rather than its shape is refused as the value it is
// (`skip=cotangent-needs-a-value`), which no shape can stand in for.
import { transpose } from transforms
import rulesets.transpose

func pick(x: float[3]): float {
    return x[1]
}

func main(): int sync {
    let back = transpose(pick)
    println(back(1.0))
    return 0
}